---
// Help: the Code ↔ Paper reader (src/components/paper/Reader.astro, src/scripts/reader*.ts;
// docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, "The Code ↔ Paper reader").
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const toc = [
  { id: "panes", text: "Two panes" },
  { id: "paper", text: "Where the paper comes from" },
  { id: "pairs", text: "Matches and their colours" },
  { id: "weak", text: "Weak matches" },
  { id: "moving", text: "Moving between matches" },
  { id: "hide", text: "Hiding the paper" },
  { id: "files", text: "The files, and the tools of a file" },
  { id: "source", text: "A copy, or the source" },
  { id: "phone", text: "On a phone" },
  { id: "keyboard", text: "Keyboard and screen readers" },
];
---
<Info
  title="The Code ↔ Paper reader"
  description={`How ${SITE_NAME}'s Code ↔ Paper reader shows a paper beside its authors' code: matches, colours, hiding the paper, the file tools.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/reader/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    The reader is the first section of a paper's page when its authors' code was read: the paper on the left, the code
    on the right, and between them the matches that {SITE_NAME} computed — which paragraph of the paper goes with which
    lines of code.
  </p>

  <h2 id="panes">Two panes</h2>
  <p>
    The reader takes the window's whole width, wider than the rest of the page, because code needs it. Each pane scrolls
    on its own. The bar above them says how many matches there are and by which method they were computed; its buttons
    move between matches and hide the paper.
  </p>

  <h2 id="paper">Where the paper comes from</h2>
  <p>
    The paper is not stored by {SITE_NAME}, nor sent by it: when the pane is shown, <strong>your browser</strong> fetches
    the paper's full text from Europe PMC (the open-access XML) and shows it as plain text, its paragraphs numbered in
    the XML's order. Europe PMC often takes a few seconds; the pane says it is loading, and when it is slow. A try waits
    20 seconds and is made once more. If Europe PMC still does not answer, a paper in PubMed Central is read from NCBI's
    copy instead, and the pane says so; its paragraphs may be numbered otherwise, so each match is placed by its section
    and its evidence terms. When neither answers, the pane keeps links to the paper at doi.org and on Europe PMC, and a
    button to try again. Without JavaScript, only those links are there.
  </p>
  <p>
    These requests go from your browser to Europe PMC (www.ebi.ac.uk) or NCBI (eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov) directly: those
    services see your address, as they would if you read the paper on their sites (<a href="/privacy/#browser">the
    privacy page</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="pairs">Matches and their colours</h2>
  <p>
    A match joins one paragraph of the paper and a range of lines of one file. Both sides take the same colour — six
    colours used in turn, so that neighbouring matches differ: a light tint on the paragraph and on its lines, with a thin
    mark in the paragraph's margin and in the code's gutter. The match you are reading turns a stronger shade, on both
    sides, with a wider mark. The rest of the code keeps its white ground and its syntax colours.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Click a coloured paragraph: its lines come into view, in the code pane.</li>
    <li>Click coloured lines: their paragraph comes into view, in the paper pane.</li>
    <li>"The matches", above the panes, unfolds the list of every match: the paragraph's section, the file and its
      lines, the score, and the evidence — a few short technical terms that both sides share (identifiers, constants,
      tool names), never a sentence of the paper. Each entry opens its match on both sides.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Matches are computed by the harvester beforehand (method <code>lexical-v1</code>): a pair is kept only when several
    rare terms agree on both sides. They are proposals, not statements by the authors; a wrong one can be reported from
    the paper's Contribute section. A paper without matches yet still shows its paper and its code side by side.
  </p>

  <h2 id="weak">Weak matches</h2>
  <p>
    When a match covers a whole file (nine tenths of its lines or more), it says only "this paragraph goes with this
    file". Such a weak match tints only the file's first line, and the list of matches marks it "a weak match".
  </p>

  <h2 id="moving">Moving between matches</h2>
  <p>
    "‹ Previous" and "Next ›" go through the matches in order, opening each on both sides. The page opens on the first
    match of the file it shows. The address follows what you read: <code>#pair-3</code> for a match,
    <code>?path=…</code> for a file, <code>#L10-L20</code> for lines, so a link you copy opens the same view.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hide">Hiding the paper</h2>
  <p>
    "Hide the paper" folds the paper pane; the code then takes the whole width. "Show the paper" brings it back. Your
    browser remembers the choice for the next papers (in its local storage, on your device only; nothing is sent).
    Hiding the paper before it has loaded also means it is not fetched.
  </p>

  <h2 id="files">The files, and the tools of a file</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Files</strong> opens or closes the list of the code's files: the matched files first, then the
      repository's folders, with a filter. A file without a copy here is in italics.</li>
    <li><strong>Wrap</strong> wraps long lines instead of scrolling sideways.</li>
    <li><strong>Copy</strong> copies the whole file; line numbers are never part of what you copy.</li>
    <li><strong>Link</strong> copies a link to the file, or to the lines you selected: click a line number, and
      Shift-click another for a range.</li>
    <li><strong>Raw</strong> downloads the file as {SITE_NAME} keeps it.</li>
    <li><strong>⋯</strong> links to the file and to its repository at the source, at the verified commit, and says why.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notebooks show their cells, their Markdown as text; the syntax is coloured by language. Email addresses written in
    the code are hidden, with a note.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source">A copy, or the source</h2>
  <p>
    A file's text is shown from {SITE_NAME}'s copy only when its repository's licence allows it. Otherwise the reader
    lists the file, says why its text is not here, and links to it — and to the exact lines of each match — at the
    source, at the verified commit. The rules, and where each copy is read from, are in the
    <PageLink href="/policies/code/">code policy</PageLink>. A file may also be withheld at a removal request; the reader
    says so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="phone">On a phone</h2>
  <p>
    On a narrow screen the panes are stacked, the code first; the list of files, when open, sits above the code. Long
    lines scroll inside the code pane, never the page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keyboard">Keyboard and screen readers</h2>
  <p>
    Each pane can be focused and scrolled with the keyboard; every button is a real button with a label, and the
    paper's loading state is announced. Colours are never the only sign of a match: the list of matches names each one
    in words. <a href="/policies/accessibility/">The accessibility statement</a> lists what is known not to work well
    yet.
  </p>
</Info>
